[UTCDate "2023.01.29"]
[UTCTime "14:19:58"]
[Orientation "white"]

{ In this chapter we will look at the main line, or most common moves from this point, on. }
1... exd4 { Exd4 remains the most common choice played over twenty five million times on lichess!
Here there are a few options for white. The most accurate and common move is Nxd4, which is what we will be looking at in this chapter but there are other moves such as c3, and Bc4, (see the next two chapters). } 2. Nxd4 { Here there are a couple ways black could respond the most accurate move as black is Nf6. } 2... Nf6 { Some other ways black could play : } (2... Nxd4?! { This is inaccurate because after Qxd4 white has plenty more development and control over the center, black is put into a uncomfortable situation. } 3. Qxd4 Qf6 (3... Nf6? 4. e5 Ng8?! { C5 is the stock fish move. } (4... c5 5. Qe3 Nd5 6. Qf3 Nc7 7. Bc4 { white has a +1.6 advantage. }) 5. Nc3) 4. Qxf6 Nxf6 5. Nc3) 3. Nxc6 bxc6 (3... dxc6?! 4. Qxd8+ Kxd8 5. f3) 4. Bd3 d5 (4... Bc5 5. Bg5 h6 6. Bh4 g5 7. Bg3 d5 8. e5) 5. exd5 cxd5 (5... Nxd5?! { This is another way for black to recapture, but is less accurate because the C-pawns are doubled, and isolated. } 6. O-O) 6. O-O Be7 { Bc5 is also very popular. } (6... Bc5?! 7. Re1+ Be6 8. Bb5+! Nd7? (8... Kf8 9. Qf3) 9. c4 O-O 10. cxd5) 7. Re1 O-O 8. h3 { h3 is to prevent Ng4, and Bc5 ideas. } { [%csl Rg4][%cal Rh3g4] } 8... Rb8 9. b3 { white according to theory needs to play Bb2, Nd2, and Qe2. } { [%csl Rb2][%cal Rc1b2] } 9... c5 10. Bb2 Be6 11. Nd2 { white plans to play Qe2 and complete their development, this is where the line stops.
Go to the next chapter to see some of the Traps. } *

[Event "Scotch: Scotch Traps"]
[Date "2023.01.29"]
[Result "*"]
[Variant "Standard"]
[ECO "C44"]
[Opening "Scotch Game: Scotch Gambit, Sarratt Variation"]
[StudyName "Scotch"]
[ChapterName "Scotch Traps"]
[ChapterURL "https://lichess.org/study/Wr4mGemS/cZLWPtY0"]
[Annotator "https://lichess.org/@/Yonushke"]
[FEN "r1bqkbnr/pppp1ppp/2n5/4p3/3PP3/5N2/PPP2PPP/RNBQKB1R b KQkq - 0 1"]
[SetUp "1"]
[UTCDate "2023.01.29"]
[UTCTime "14:39:03"]
[Orientation "white"]

{ In This chapter we will look at all the traps your opponent can fall into in the scotch. }
1... exd4 { After black plays Exd4 there are some traps with Bc4, C3, and Ng5, lets look at all of them. } 2. Bc4 (2. c3 { This is called the Goring Gambit, but I assure you, it isn't boring, white doesn't bother to take back on d4. } 2... dxc3 3. Bc4 { White still doesn't take back. } 3... cxb2 4. Bxb2 { look at these bishops! } { [%cal Rb2h8,Rc4g8] } 4... Bb4+ (4... Bc5?? { obviously a blunder. Bxg7 wins a rook. } 5. Bxg7 d6 6. Bxh8) 5. Nc3 Nf6 6. O-O { This isn't a trap but it is a nice way to play. Even though white is down two pawns the position is equal because of their extra development and control over the center. }) 2... Bc5 (2... Nf6 3. Ng5 { [%csl Rf7][%cal Rc4f7,Rg5f7] } 3... d5 4. exd5 Nxd5 5. O-O Be7 6. Nxf7!! Kxf7 7. Qh5+! g6 8. Bxd5+ Ke8 9. Qf3 { White is completely winning here. }) 3. Ng5 { White attacks the f7 pawn. } { [%csl Rf7][%cal Rg5f7,Rc4f7] } (3. c3 { This is known as the Haxo Gambit, it has the same idea as the goring gambit } 3... dxc3 (3... Nf6 { Best is Nf6, black should calmly give the pawn back to avoid any trouble. } 4. cxd4 (4. e5 d5 5. Bb5 Ne4 6. cxd4)) 4. Bxf7+!! (4. Qd5 { Qd5 has the same idea but doesn't work because of Qe7. } 4... Qe7 { [%csl Gc5,Gf7][%cal Ge7f7,Ge7c5] }) 4... Kxf7 5. Qd5+! { [%csl Rc5,Rf7][%cal Rd5c5,Rd5f7] } 5... Ke8 6. Qh5+ Kf8 7. Qxc5+ Qe7 8. Qxe7+ Ngxe7 9. Nxc3 { White has a more active position, they can continue with Bf4 and long castles (o-o-o). }) 3... Nh6 (3... Ne5 4. Nxf7!! Nxf7 5. Bxf7+ Kxf7 6. Qh5+! g6 7. Qxc5 { White won the piece back and is going to take the black pawn on d4, there is no way for black to defend against that. }) 4. Nxf7!! Nxf7 5. Bxf7+ Kxf7 6. Qh5+ g6 7. Qxc5 { Material is equal, and the position is also balanced, white can continue with o-o and Bg5. } *

[Event "Scotch: A similar gambit"]
[Date "2023.01.30"]
[Result "*"]
[Variant "Standard"]
