[Opening "Danish Gambit Accepted"]
[StudyName "Scotch"]
[ChapterName "A similar gambit"]
[ChapterURL "https://lichess.org/study/Wr4mGemS/eHbpo7K2"]
[Annotator "https://lichess.org/@/Yonushke"]
[UTCDate "2023.01.30"]
[UTCTime "21:57:11"]
[Orientation "white"]

{ In this chapter we will look at some of the similar gambits that are related to the Scotch Gambit. }
1. e4 e5 2. d4 { Here I will show you the Danish Gambit. } { [%csl Rd4][%cal Re5d4] } 2... exd4 { white sacrifices some pawns here. } { [%cal Gc2c3] } 3. c3 { [%csl Rc3][%cal Rd4c3] } 3... dxc3 4. Bc4! { white just keeps developing. } 4... cxb2 5. Bxb2 { This is the end of the Danish gambit, the point is that white is down two pawns but in exchange has a lot of development and control over the center, black has not developed any pieces yet! Here I will show you a fun trap, if black gets to greedy. } { [%cal Rb2h8,Rc4g8] } 5... Qg5 { You see what I mean? black on the very next move attacks g2, TO GREEDY!! } { [%csl Rg2][%cal Rg5g2] } 6. Nf3! { White has already sacrificed two pawns, so they don't care about sacrificing another one in exchange for development. } 6... Qxg2 { Black greedily snatches another pawn of white's but now it is to late, black can't avoid the trap. } 7. Rg1! Qh3 8. Bxf7+!! { look at this, Kxf7, Ng5+ wins the queen. } 8... Kxf7 (8... Kd8 9. Rg3! Qh6 10. Nc3 Nf6 (10... c6 11. Qb3 Na6 (11... Ne7 12. Ng5 Na6 13. O-O-O Rb8 14. Kb1 Nc5 15. Qc2 { white has a crushing postion, also black has to be EXTREMELY careful, because if black's knight on c5 ever moves Ne6 is going to be a big problem for black. } { [%csl Re6][%cal Rg5e6] }) 12. Bxg8) 11. Bc1 { Black's queen is trapped here as well. } { [%cal Rc1h6,Rf7h5] }) 9. Ng5+ { [%csl Rh3,Rf7][%cal Rg5f7,Rg5h3] } 9... Ke8 10. Nxh3 { There you go, white is up a queen, and can easily win this. } *

[Event "Scotch: Scotch game, Potter Variation"]
[Date "2023.02.03"]
[Result "*"]
[Variant "Standard"]
[ECO "C45"]
[Opening "Scotch Game: Potter Variation"]
[StudyName "Scotch"]
[ChapterName "Scotch game, Potter Variation"]
[ChapterURL "https://lichess.org/study/Wr4mGemS/4ttgAJqY"]
[Annotator "https://lichess.org/@/Yonushke"]
[UTCDate "2023.02.03"]
[UTCTime "00:44:56"]
[Orientation "white"]

{ In this chapter I will show you another variation of the scotch opening, that most players don't know. (btw, this is actually my secret opening prep) }
1. e4 e5 2. Nf3 Nc6 3. d4 exd4 4. Nxd4 Bc5 5. Nb3 { This Nb3 move looks strange at first, but this is the start to a very interesting opening called the potter variation, from here I will show you some variations that could happen here } 5... Bb6 (5... Bb4+ 6. c3 Be7 7. Bb5) 6. Nc3 { [%csl Gc3,Gb3] } 6... d6 (6... Nf6 7. Qe2 O-O 8. Be3 d5 9. O-O-O d4 { even though this is a fork, since black's d-pawn is pinned the pawn is not threatening anything. } { [%cal Rd4c3,Rd4e3] } 10. Nb5 { [%cal Rb5d4,Rb3d4,Re3d4,Rd1d4] } 10... Nxe4 11. N3xd4 Nxd4 12. Nxd4) (6... Nge7 7. Qe2 O-O 8. Be3 d5 9. O-O-O Be6?? { here, white has a fun tactic. } 10. exd5 Nxd5 (10... Bxd5 11. Nxd5 Nxd5 12. c4 { still winning a piece. } { [%cal Rc4d5,Rd1d8] }) 11. Nxd5 Bxd5 12. c4 { white is going to win a piece. } { [%cal Rc4d5,Rd1d8] }) 7. Qe2 { This is yet another strange looking move. Qe2 you might say, blocks the light squared bishop on f1, but according to theory this is the way for white to continue, the plan is Be3, f3, long castles, and then an attack on the king side. } 7... Nf6 8. Be3 { [%cal Re3b6,Rb6e3] } 8... O-O (8... Bxe3 9. Qxe3 { Black is only helping white develop their bishop, by trading. } { [%cal Rf1b5,Ge2e3] } 9... O-O 10. f3 Be6 11. O-O-O Bxb3 { [%cal Gc2b3] } 12. cxb3 a5 { black will often try to attack the queen side, this can easily be stopped with h3, NOT H4, the reason h4, is dangerous is because black's knight has acess to b4. } 13. a3 { [%cal Ga3b4] }) 9. O-O-O d5 { A LOT of people will play d5 here, but this is dangerous for black. } { [%cal Rd1d8] } (9... Be6 10. f3 { [%cal Ge6b3,Gc2b3,Rg2e4] } 10... Qd7 11. g4! { White begins the attack quickly! Some players will play h6, but this only makes things worse for black. } 11... h6 12. g5 hxg5 13. Bxg5 Nh7 14. h4 { [%cal Rh4g5] } 14... Nxg5 15. hxg5 { [%cal Rh1h8] } 15... f6 { with f6 black is trying to give the king some space to run away. but white just plays g3, stopping EVERYTHING. } { [%cal Gg8f7] } 16. g6 { [%cal Rg6f7,Rg6h7] } 16... Rfd8 { [%cal Rg8f8] } 17. Qh2 { here it is game over, there is no way to stop mate. } { [%cal Rh2h8] } 17... Kf8 18. Qh8+ Ke7 19. Qxg7+ Ke8 (19... Bf7 20. Qxf7#) 20. Rh8+ Bg8 21. Rxg8# { oof! }) (9... Qe7 { [%cal Re7e4,Rf6e4] } 10. f3 Be6 11. g4 { [%cal Gg4g5] } 11... Nd7 12. h4 a5 { [%cal Ga5a4] } 13. a3 a4 14. Nd2 { [%cal Rg4g5,Rh4h5] } 14... Rfe8 15. g5 { white's next goal is to push the h pawn to h6, if g6, white goes h6 anyway. } { [%csl Rh6][%cal Rh4h6] } 15... g6 16. h5 gxh5 (16... Qf8 { [%cal Ge7f8,Gf8g7] } 17. f4 { [%cal Gf4g5] } 17... Ra5 { [%cal Ga5h5] } 18. Bh3 Bxh3 19. Rxh3 { in almost every variation white has a crushing attack. } { [%cal Rd1h1,Rh5g6,Rh7g6,Rh3h8,Rh1h8] }) 17. Bxb6 Nxb6 18. Rxh5 { [%cal Re2h2,Rh5h7] }) 10. exd5 Nxd5 11. Nxd5 { this is very good for white, this is 100% winning. } *

